<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joséphine de Beauharnais</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1763–1814</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Empress Joséphine</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joséphine de Beauharnais was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/les-trois-ilets">Trois-Îlets</a> on Martinique into a planter family. Widowed when her husband Alexandre de Beauharnais was guillotined in 1794, she married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a> in 1796 and became Empress of the French in 1804, until their divorce in 1810. Her circle helped some German princely houses, such as Hohenzollern-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmaringen">Sigmaringen</a>, survive the mediatisation of 1806.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/josephine-de-beauharnais.jpg" alt="Joséphine de Beauharnais" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Baron François Gérard (1770 - 1837) – Painter (French) Born in Rome. Died in Paris. Details on Google Art Project · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baron_Fran%C3%A7ois_G%C3%A9rard_-_Jos%C3%A9phine_in_coronation_costume_-_Google_Art_Project_(cropped,_Josephine_detail).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
